So the static `xmlbase` in readclause.c is only set to `NULL` in `InitText2` and initialized to `""`. It's passed by value to `ProcessSsmlTag`. So the static `xmlbase` never points into `namedata`.

Wait, but the local `xmlbase` in `ProcessSsmlTag` is set to `&namedata[index]` at line 895. This is a local copy. So within a single call, if the tag is `<speak xml:base="...">`, the local `xmlbase` points into `namedata`. But it's not used again in that same call.

Hmm, so where's the use-after-free? Let me reconsider. 

Actually, wait. Let me reconsider the `uri` pointer in the SSML_AUDIO case. `uri = &namedata[index]`. This is used in `uri_callback(1, uri, xmlbase)`. But `uri_callback` is NULL in the fuzzer.

Let me reconsider. Maybe the vulnerability is in the `namedata` handling with the marker names. Let me look at the SSML_MARK case:

```c
case SSML_MARK:
        if ((attr1 = GetSsmlAttribute(px, "name")) != NULL) {
                attrcopy_utf8(buf, attr1, sizeof(buf));
                if (strcmp(skip_marker, buf) == 0) {
                        ...
                }
                if ((index = AddNameData(buf, 0)) >= 0) {
                        sprintf(buf, "%c%dM", CTRL_EMBEDDED, index);
                        ...
                }
        }
```

This adds marker names to `namedata`. And in speech.c line 527: `ep->id.name = &namedata[value]`. So marker names are stored as pointers into `namedata`. If `namedata` is realloced after the marker is added, then `ep->id.name` becomes dangling.

Let me look at speech.c around line 527.

